SCROWLL ANIMATION TRANSITION
Build a premium scroll-driven transition between the hero section and the next section. Add a fixed, pointer-events-none transition layer placed after the hero markup and before the next section. The layer must stay hidden by default and become visible only while the user is scrolling down through the hero-to-next-section boundary. Do not show this transition when the user scrolls up.

Use GIF assets for the transition instead of CSS-drawn birds. Add multiple duplicated GIF elements so the screen feels full and cinematic, using assets like b1.gif, p2.gif, leaves.gif, pegion.gif, and leafe.gif. Use about four copies of the GIF group, around 20 total moving elements, with each element having a unique class for position, scale, opacity, travel distance, and timing variation. Make sure the GIF files are available in the public/static assets folder so the live website can load them.

The animation direction must feel like the GIFs enter from the right/top side and travel toward the left/down side while scrolling down. Tilt each GIF from the top-right corner using transform-origin: 100% 0 and rotate around -40deg, so the left edge appears raised higher. The movement must be controlled by a CSS variable such as --transition-progress that is updated from JavaScript based on scroll position.

The JavaScript should calculate the hero bottom and next-section top, define a long scroll range around that boundary, clamp the progress from 0 to 1, and ease it with smoothstep: progress * progress * (3 - 2 * progress). Use requestAnimationFrame for scroll updates. Track scroll direction with lastY/currentY and only activate the layer when direction is downward and progress is inside the active range. On upward scroll, immediately remove active classes so the GIF animation does not appear.

The CSS should map --transition-progress into translate3d and scale values. Start the GIFs outside the right side of the viewport, stagger some farther out with larger start-x values, and move them across the screen with different travel-x and travel-y values. Keep motion smooth and consistent by using a longer scroll range in JavaScript and a short transform transition, such as 200ms to 240ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1). Avoid fast rushing. The animation should feel slow, elegant, polished, and connected directly to the user's scroll.

The transition layer must sit in front of the hero and next section without blocking clicks. Use a high z-index below navigation if needed, overflow hidden, opacity/visibility transitions, and responsive clamp sizes for GIF widths. Keep the existing hero, gallery, wishes, songs, and other page sections unchanged. The final result should be a smooth, premium, birthday-style nature transition where animated GIF birds/leaves glide across the hero boundary only on downward scroll.
